# Docker Compose variables for a SERVER deployment — the shared, untenanted
# half, serving every tenant:
#
#   docker compose --env-file config/server.env \
#       -f modules/event-bus/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/backend/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/eliza/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/vad-asr/docker-compose.yml \
#       -f modules/emissor/docker-compose.yml up -d --wait --pull always
#
# Only values Compose itself needs before any container exists belong in
# here: image tags, published ports and bind addresses, the project name.
# Everything the application reads — which facets are switched off, and the
# fact that this deployment is untenanted — lives in config/custom.config.
#
# Nothing here is required for the command above: every value is the compose
# files' own default, repeated so it is visible. The reverse-proxy block at
# the bottom is the exception — caddy refuses to start without it.

# Compose project name, and the name of the Docker network every module
# joins. The default is also the name custom-module/ joins as an external
# network, so leave it alone unless you are running two servers on one
# machine.
# CLTL_PROJECT=cltl-platform

# Pin every image to one tag instead of :latest.
# VERSION=1.0.0

# Published host ports.
# CLTL_BACKEND_PORT=8001      # storage API, what each tenant forwards to
#
# Not configurable here: modules/emissor publishes 8002 and modules/event-bus
# publishes 5672 (AMQP) and 15672 (management UI) on fixed host ports. AMQP
# must stay reachable from every tenant machine — on 5672, or over AMQPS on
# 5671 when modules/reverse-proxy is in the bring-up command.

# The event bus's admin password (user eliza). Applied when storage/rabbitmq
# is first initialised, so it must match [cltl.event.kombu] server in
# config/custom.config from the start; scripts/install-server.sh generates
# and sets both.
# CLTL_RABBITMQ_PASS=eliza123

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only when modules/reverse-proxy is part of the bring-up command
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required by caddy — it refuses to start without both. The DNS name must
# already resolve to this host, with port 80 reachable, before the first
# `up`: Caddy's ACME HTTP-01 challenge needs it. See
# doc/DEPLOYMENT.md#fronting-a-public-server-with-tls-and-auth.
# CLTL_HOSTNAME=combot.example.org
# CLTL_ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org

# The ACME directory caddy requests its certificate from; Let's Encrypt
# production when unset. The staging directory avoids its rate limits:
# CLTL_ACME_CA=https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory

# Once caddy is serving, set these to 127.0.0.1 and re-run `up`: it stops the
# raw storage, EMISSOR, management-UI and plain AMQP ports from being
# reachable from outside this host at all, leaving caddy (443) and AMQPS
# (5671) as the only ways in. Tenants then connect with
# amqps://<user>:<password>@<CLTL_HOSTNAME>:5671/.
# CLTL_STORAGE_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CLTL_EMISSOR_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CLTL_RABBITMQ_MGMT_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CLTL_AMQP_BIND=127.0.0.1
